def R(t,u): return {"title":t,"url":u}
def Q(q,c,a,w): return {"q":q,"choices":c,"answer":a,"why":w}
def T(id,title,expl,subs):
    return {"id":id,"title":title,"explainer":expl,"subtopics":[{"id":f"{id}-s{i+1}","title":s} for i,s in enumerate(subs)]}
def S(id,title,why,expl,res,project,topics,quiz,concepts):
    return {"id":id,"title":title,"why":why,"explainer":expl,"resources":res,"project":project,"topics":topics,"quiz":quiz,"conceptIds":concepts}

# (track, level_id, subject)
NEW=[
("infra","f-l2",S("f-interconnect","GPU Interconnects & Collective Debugging",
"Most multi-GPU slowdowns are communication problems, not compute problems.",
"Inside a node, GPUs talk over NVLink/NVSwitch; between nodes, over InfiniBand or RoCE with GPUDirect RDMA. NCCL turns that fabric into collectives (all-reduce, all-gather, all-to-all). Your job is to know the expected bandwidth of every hop, prove it with nccl-tests, and recognize when a job is slow because a link, NIC binding or transport choice is wrong.\n\nCommon mistakes: tensor parallelism across nodes; GPU and NIC on different CPU sockets; NCCL silently falling back to TCP sockets; ignoring link error counters until a job hangs.\n\nMental model: compute is rented by the hour, but you are often paying for GPUs that wait on the network.",
[R("NCCL troubleshooting","https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/nccl/user-guide/docs/troubleshooting.html"),R("NCCL environment variables","https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/nccl/user-guide/docs/env.html"),R("nccl-tests (GitHub)","https://github.com/NVIDIA/nccl-tests"),R("GPUDirect RDMA","https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/gpudirect-rdma/index.html")],
"Run all_reduce_perf on one node and across two nodes. Record busbw, compare with link specs, then deliberately break the IB binding (wrong NCCL_IB_HCA) and document how the logs and numbers change.",
[T("f-ic-topo","Fabric Topology","Topology decides achievable bandwidth. Read nvidia-smi topo -m: NV# means NVLink between GPUs, PIX/PXB means PCIe switches, SYS means crossing CPU sockets (slow). Each GPU should have a nearby NIC (rail-optimized designs pair GPU i on every node with the same switch rail). Know your numbers: NVLink on H100 is about 900 GB/s bidirectional per GPU, a 400 Gb/s NIC is 50 GB/s per direction, PCIe Gen5 x16 about 64 GB/s per direction. Plan placement so the heaviest traffic, usually tensor parallelism, stays on NVLink.",["nvidia-smi topo -m reading","NVLink / NVSwitch domains","Rail-optimized fabrics","GPU-NIC affinity and NUMA","InfiniBand vs RoCE trade-offs"]),
 T("f-ic-nccl","NCCL Collectives","NCCL chooses algorithm (ring, tree, NVLS) and transport (NVLink, IB verbs, sockets) per collective. Ring all-reduce sends 2(N-1)/N of the buffer per GPU; tree lowers latency for small messages; NVLink SHARP offloads reductions into NVSwitch. nccl-tests reports algbw and busbw: compare busbw with link speed to judge efficiency. Message size matters: small messages are latency-bound, large ones bandwidth-bound, so always sweep sizes. NCCL_DEBUG=INFO prints the chosen rings, channels and transports; that log is the first thing to read when performance looks wrong.",["All-reduce, all-gather, reduce-scatter, all-to-all","Ring vs tree vs NVLS","algbw vs busbw","Message-size sweeps","NCCL_DEBUG logs"]),
 T("f-ic-debug","Debugging Hangs & Slowdowns","Slow or hung collectives usually come from one of a few causes: a straggler rank (bad GPU, thermal throttling, noisy neighbor), a degraded link (rising symbol errors or link flaps on IB ports), transport fallback to sockets, wrong interface selection, or ranks calling collectives in different orders. Tools: NCCL_DEBUG=INFO and NCCL_DEBUG_SUBSYS, ibstat and perfquery for port counters, DCGM for NVLink errors, PyTorch's NCCL timeouts and flight recorder to find which rank is behind. Bisect: single node first, then pairs of nodes, to isolate the bad component.",["Straggler detection","IB port counters (ibstat, perfquery)","Socket fallback symptoms","Flight recorder and timeouts","Node-pair bisection"])],
[Q("nccl-tests busbw far below link speed most likely indicates…",["Perfect performance","A topology, transport or placement problem","Too much HBM","A tokenizer bug"],1,"busbw is normalized to compare with hardware link rates."),
 Q("In nvidia-smi topo -m, 'SYS' between a GPU and NIC means…",["Same PCIe switch","Traffic crosses the CPU interconnect between sockets","NVLink","No connection"],1,"SYS paths are the slowest; pick a NIC on the GPU's socket."),
 Q("Ring all-reduce traffic per GPU is approximately…",["N × buffer","2(N-1)/N × buffer","buffer / N","Zero with NVLink"],1,"It stays near 2× buffer regardless of GPU count."),
 Q("A good first step for a multi-node hang is…",["Retrain the model","Find the lagging rank with timeouts/flight recorder and check that node's logs","Add more nodes","Lower temperature"],1,"Hangs usually trace to one rank or link.")],
["nccl-collectives","xid-triage"])),

("infra","f-l2",S("f-gpushare","GPU Sharing & Batch Scheduling on Kubernetes",
"GPUs are too expensive to leave idle or to hand out one whole card at a time.",
"The GPU Operator turns Kubernetes nodes into GPU nodes: driver, container toolkit, device plugin, DCGM exporter, MIG manager and feature discovery. On top, you choose how to share: MIG for hard isolation, time-slicing for dev density, MPS for concurrent kernels. DRA gives pods richer device requests. Kueue and Volcano add the queueing, quotas, fair share and gang scheduling that batch and training jobs need.\n\nCommon mistakes: time-slicing production inference (no memory isolation); no quotas, so one team grabs every GPU; running distributed jobs without gang scheduling, causing partial-allocation deadlocks.\n\nGoal: high utilization with predictable performance for the workloads that need it.",
[R("NVIDIA GPU Operator","https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/cloud-native/gpu-operator/latest/index.html"),R("MIG user guide","https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/tesla/mig-user-guide/"),R("Kubernetes Dynamic Resource Allocation","https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/dynamic-resource-allocation/"),R("Kueue overview","https://kueue.sigs.k8s.io/docs/overview/"),R("Volcano docs","https://volcano.sh/en/docs/")],
"On a GPU node, install the GPU Operator, enable time-slicing with 4 replicas, then switch a second GPU to a MIG mixed profile. Run the same small model on both and compare latency under a noisy neighbor. Add Kueue with two team queues and show fair sharing.",
[T("f-gs-operator","GPU Operator & Device Stack","The GPU Operator manages the whole node-side stack as Kubernetes resources: driver containers (or pre-installed drivers), the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, the device plugin advertising nvidia.com/gpu, GPU Feature Discovery labels (model, memory, MIG capability), the DCGM exporter for metrics, and the MIG manager. Upgrades roll through nodes like any DaemonSet, which is why driver upgrades need maintenance windows and drains. Node labels from feature discovery let you schedule by GPU model or memory size instead of hard-coding node names.",["Operator components","Driver containers vs host drivers","Feature discovery labels","DCGM exporter","Upgrade and drain strategy"]),
 T("f-gs-share","MIG, Time-Slicing & MPS","MIG partitions supported GPUs (A100, H100, newer) into isolated instances with dedicated SMs and memory; profiles like 1g.10gb or 3g.40gb are fixed, and changing them requires the GPU to be idle. Time-slicing advertises a GPU as several replicas with no isolation, ideal for notebooks and CI. MPS lets processes run kernels concurrently with optional SM limits, improving throughput for many small inference workers. In the GPU Operator these are configured via ConfigMaps and node labels. Pick by isolation need: production multi-tenant means MIG; dev density means time-slicing.",["MIG profiles and strategies","Time-slicing replicas","MPS for small workers","Isolation and noisy neighbors","Choosing per workload"]),
 T("f-gs-dra","Dynamic Resource Allocation","Device plugins only expose integer counts. DRA introduces DeviceClasses and ResourceClaims so a pod can ask for devices by attributes (GPU model, memory, MIG profile, interconnect) and share a claimed device between containers. Drivers publish ResourceSlices describing available devices; the scheduler matches claims against them, optionally with CEL selectors. DRA graduated to general availability in recent Kubernetes releases, and NVIDIA ships a DRA driver. Expect it to become the standard way to request accelerators, including multi-node NVLink domains.",["ResourceClaims and DeviceClasses","ResourceSlices","CEL device selectors","NVIDIA DRA driver","Migration from device plugins"]),
 T("f-gs-queue","Kueue & Volcano","Batch jobs need queues, not first-come scheduling. Kueue adds ClusterQueues with quotas per resource flavor (for example H100 versus L4), LocalQueues per team, borrowing of unused quota, preemption, and all-or-nothing admission for gang-style jobs. Volcano is a batch scheduler with gang scheduling, queues and fair share, popular for Spark, MPI and training. Both prevent the classic deadlock where two distributed jobs each hold half the GPUs they need. Pair them with PriorityClasses so production serving can preempt experiments.",["ClusterQueue / LocalQueue","Resource flavors and borrowing","Gang (all-or-nothing) admission","Volcano queues and fair share","Preemption policies"])],
[Q("Which sharing mode gives hardware memory isolation between tenants?",["Time-slicing","MIG","MPS default","Overcommit"],1,"MIG instances have dedicated memory and SMs."),
 Q("DRA improves on device plugins by…",["Removing GPUs","Allowing attribute-based device requests via ResourceClaims","Disabling scheduling","Replacing containers"],1,"Pods can ask for specific device properties."),
 Q("Gang scheduling prevents…",["High utilization","Two distributed jobs each holding partial GPUs and deadlocking","Logging","MIG"],1,"All-or-nothing admission avoids partial allocations."),
 Q("Kueue resource flavors let you…",["Change GPU colors","Quota different GPU types separately","Skip quotas","Install drivers"],1,"Flavors represent distinct hardware pools.")],
["gpu-sharing"])),

("infra","f-l3",S("f-failops","GPU Failure Operations",
"At scale, GPUs fail weekly; the skill is turning failures into routine operations.",
"Large clusters see constant hardware faults: memory ECC errors, GPUs falling off the bus, NVLink errors, overheating, and the hangs they cause in collective jobs. Good GPU operations read Xid codes, run DCGM diagnostics, drain and reset automatically, file RMAs with evidence, and keep training jobs resilient through checkpoints and spares.\n\nCommon mistakes: rescheduling jobs onto a node that just threw a fatal Xid; no health check between jobs; checkpoint intervals so long that each failure costs hours.\n\nHalf of AI SRE work lives here.",
[R("NVIDIA Xid errors","https://docs.nvidia.com/deploy/xid-errors/index.html"),R("DCGM user guide","https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/dcgm/latest/user-guide/index.html"),R("GPU memory error management","https://docs.nvidia.com/deploy/a100-gpu-mem-error-mgmt/index.html"),R("PyTorch distributed elastic","https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/distributed.elastic.html")],
"Write a node health controller: watch dmesg for Xids and DCGM health, classify them, cordon and drain on fatal codes, run dcgmi diag level 2 before returning the node, and open a ticket with logs on repeats. Test it with simulated log lines.",
[T("f-fo-xid","Xid & ECC Errors","Xid events are driver-reported faults in the kernel log. Some point at applications (13 graphics engine exception, 31 MMU fault), others at hardware: 48 double-bit ECC, 63/64 row remapping events, 74 NVLink error, 79 GPU fell off the bus, 92 high single-bit ECC rate, 94/95 contained and uncontained ECC errors on recent GPUs. Modern GPUs remap bad memory rows and need a reset to apply remaps; nvidia-smi -q shows remapped rows and pending remaps. Decide per class: investigate the app, reset, drain, or RMA.",["Reading dmesg / journalctl","Application vs hardware Xids","ECC, row remapping and resets","NVLink errors","When to RMA"]),
 T("f-fo-hang","NCCL Hangs & Stragglers","A collective waits for every rank, so one slow or dead GPU stalls thousands. Symptoms: step time spikes, all GPUs idle or spinning, no progress logs. Turn on NCCL and PyTorch timeouts so hangs become errors, enable the flight recorder to dump which collective each rank is in, and compare: the rank that is behind is the suspect. Check its node for Xids, thermal throttling or link errors. Stragglers that are slow but alive show up as consistent per-rank step time outliers; track them as a metric.",["Collective timeouts","Flight recorder dumps","Straggler metrics","Thermal and power throttling","Link flaps"]),
 T("f-fo-auto","Remediation Automation","Manual triage does not scale past a few hundred GPUs. Automate a loop: detect (Xid watcher, DCGM health, failed jobs), classify, act (cordon, drain, GPU reset, node reboot), verify (DCGM diagnostics, a short burn-in or nccl-test), and return or escalate to RMA with logs attached. Keep spare nodes so jobs restart immediately. Measure mean time to detect and repair, and the share of GPU-hours lost to failures; that number justifies investment in checkpoints and spares.",["Detect-classify-act loop","Cordon / drain / reset","DCGM diag and burn-in","Spare pools","MTTR and lost GPU-hours"])],
[Q("Xid 79 usually means…",["A Python exception","The GPU fell off the bus; drain the node","Normal operation","Low disk"],1,"It is a fatal hardware/PCIe condition."),
 Q("Repeated uncontained ECC errors on one GPU should lead to…",["Ignoring them","Draining and RMA","Lower batch size","More logging only"],1,"Repeated memory faults indicate failing hardware."),
 Q("To find the culprit in an NCCL hang, look for…",["The fastest rank","The rank behind in the collective sequence","The largest model","The oldest log"],1,"Flight recorder dumps show which rank lags."),
 Q("Before returning a repaired node to the pool you should…",["Skip checks","Run DCGM diagnostics or a burn-in test","Reinstall the OS always","Change its name"],1,"Verification prevents repeat failures on live jobs.")],
["xid-triage","nccl-collectives"])),

("infra","f-l3",S("f-aisec","AI-Specific Security for Platforms",
"AI adds threats classic infra security does not cover: injected instructions, stolen weights, over-permissioned agents.",
"Platform teams own the controls that make AI applications safe regardless of model behavior. That means defending against prompt injection architecturally, protecting model weights as crown-jewel assets, scoping agent and tool permissions tightly, and governing MCP servers as supply chain.\n\nCommon mistakes: giving agents a shared admin token; letting agents fetch arbitrary URLs (an exfiltration channel); storing weights on broadly readable buckets; installing community MCP servers without review.\n\nSecurity for AI is mostly classic least privilege applied to new actors.",
[R("OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"),R("MCP security best practices","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/draft/basic/security_best_practices"),R("RAND: Securing AI Model Weights","https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RRA2849-1.html"),R("MITRE ATLAS","https://atlas.mitre.org/"),R("Simon Willison: the lethal trifecta","https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/")],
"Threat-model an internal agent that reads tickets, queries a database and posts to Slack. Map it to the lethal trifecta, then implement controls: scoped per-user tokens, an egress allowlist, an approval gate on posts, and a pinned MCP manifest. Red-team it with an injected ticket.",
[T("f-as-inject","Prompt Injection Defense in Depth","Treat every model input that is not from the authenticated user as hostile, including retrieved documents, web pages, emails and tool results. Since detection is imperfect, limit consequences: remove one leg of the lethal trifecta (untrusted input, private data, outbound channel) wherever possible; block arbitrary egress, including rendered markdown images; require approval for outbound and irreversible actions; separate a privileged planner from a quarantined reader that returns only typed values; and log tool calls for detection. Test with an injection corpus in CI.",["Direct vs indirect injection","Lethal trifecta analysis","Egress and markdown-image exfiltration","Dual-LLM / quarantined reader","Injection regression tests"]),
 T("f-as-weights","Weight Protection & Exfiltration","Model weights can represent tens of millions of dollars and, for frontier models, real misuse risk. Protect them like signing keys: encrypt at rest with tightly scoped keys, restrict who and what can read buckets, keep inference in network segments without broad egress, monitor for large unusual reads and transfers, and use confidential computing where the host is not fully trusted. Insider risk matters: limit direct access and require multi-party approval for exports. Also watch model extraction via the API, where heavy query patterns imitate the model.",["Encryption and key scoping","Bucket and registry access","Egress monitoring for large transfers","Confidential computing","Insider risk and API extraction"]),
 T("f-as-agentperm","Agent, Tool & MCP Permissions","Agents act with whatever credentials you give them, so scope them to the end user and the task: per-user OAuth tokens, short lifetimes, read-only by default, separate tools for reads and writes. Classify tools by side effect and attach policy (approval, rate limits) per class. For MCP, review servers like dependencies, pin versions and tool manifests to detect silent changes, run servers in sandboxes with egress limits, and centralize auth and logging in a gateway. Never let a server receive tokens meant for another service.",["Per-user scoped tokens","Side-effect classes and approvals","MCP server review and pinning","Sandboxed MCP servers","Gateway policy and audit"])],
[Q("The 'lethal trifecta' combines…",["GPU, CPU, RAM","Untrusted input, private data access, and outbound communication","Three models","Three regions"],1,"Together they allow data exfiltration via injection."),
 Q("Why block agents from rendering arbitrary markdown images?",["Images are slow","Image URLs can carry stolen data to an attacker's server","Licensing","Accessibility"],1,"A fetched URL with data in the query string is an exfil channel."),
 Q("Pinning an MCP tool manifest defends against…",["Latency","A server silently changing a tool's description or behavior","Token costs","GPU faults"],1,"Changes are detected before the model sees them."),
 Q("Model weights should be treated like…",["Public docs","Crown-jewel secrets such as signing keys","Temp files","Logs"],1,"They are high-value assets for theft and misuse.")],
["prompt-injection-defense"])),

("infra","f-l4",S("f-econ","AI Unit Economics & Capacity Planning",
"'Watch costs' is not a skill; computing $/1M tokens and GPUs-per-SLO is.",
"AI infrastructure decisions come down to a few numbers: dollars per million tokens at a given latency SLO, GPUs needed for peak load with headroom, and the power and cooling those GPUs require. This subject teaches the arithmetic and the measurements behind it, so you can compare self-hosting with APIs, size clusters, and explain spend to finance.\n\nCommon mistakes: using peak benchmark throughput instead of throughput at your SLO; ignoring utilization; forgetting redundancy, engineering time and power limits.",
[R("vLLM benchmarking docs","https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/contributing/benchmarks.html"),R("NVIDIA NIM LLM benchmarking guide","https://docs.nvidia.com/nim/benchmarking/llm/latest/index.html"),R("FinOps Foundation: FinOps for AI","https://www.finops.org/wg/finops-for-ai-overview/"),R("Uptime Institute research","https://uptimeinstitute.com/resources/research-and-reports")],
"Benchmark one model at three latency SLOs, compute $/1M tokens for each at 40%, 70% and 90% utilization, compare with two API prices, and write a one-page sizing memo for 200 req/s peak including GPUs, nodes and kW.",
[T("f-ec-cpt","Cost per Million Tokens","For self-hosted serving: $/1M tokens = GPU $/hour ÷ (tokens/hour ÷ 1,000,000). Measure tokens/hour at your latency SLO with realistic input and output lengths; throughput at batch 256 with 10-second latency is irrelevant if you promise 50 ms per token. Separate input and output tokens, as APIs do, since prefill is far cheaper per token than decode. Then divide by utilization to get the real cost of useful tokens. Add overheads: idle redundancy, engineering, storage, networking and eval traffic.",["Throughput at SLO","Input vs output token cost","Utilization adjustment","Overheads and redundancy","API price comparison"]),
 T("f-ec-cap","Capacity Planning","Work backwards from demand. Required output tokens/s = peak requests/s × average output tokens. GPUs = required ÷ measured per-GPU tokens/s at SLO, plus headroom for spikes, failures and deploys (commonly 20-40%), rounded up to whole nodes and to your parallelism layout. Check KV memory too: concurrency × context length × KV bytes per token must fit. Revisit monthly, because model swaps, quantization and prompt length changes move every number.",["Demand modeling","Per-GPU throughput at SLO","Headroom policy","KV memory check","Monthly re-planning"]),
 T("f-ec-power","Power & Cooling Budgets","GPUs turn into kilowatts. An 8-GPU H100 server can draw around 10 kW; newer rack-scale systems exceed 100 kW per rack and need liquid cooling. Facility constraints, utility capacity, rack power and cooling type, often decide where and how fast you can grow. Track energy per million tokens alongside cost, and include power headroom in capacity plans: you cannot add servers to a rack that is already at its power limit.",["Server and rack power draw","Air vs liquid cooling limits","Facility and utility lead times","Energy per million tokens","Power headroom in plans"])],
[Q("A GPU at $3/h producing 6M useful tokens per hour costs per 1M tokens…",["$0.18","$0.50","$2.00","$18"],1,"$3 ÷ 6 = $0.50."),
 Q("Why measure throughput at your latency SLO?",["It is higher","Max-batch throughput may violate the SLO you promise","It is required by law","It avoids KV"],1,"Only SLO-compliant throughput is sellable."),
 Q("Peak 100 req/s × 400 output tokens, 2,000 tokens/s per GPU, 25% headroom needs…",["20 GPUs","25 GPUs","40 GPUs","50 GPUs"],1,"40,000 ÷ 2,000 = 20; × 1.25 = 25."),
 Q("A rack at its power limit means…",["Add more servers anyway","No more servers until power or cooling is upgraded","Use bigger GPUs freely","Lower PUE automatically"],1,"Power, not floor space, is often the binding constraint.")],
["cost-per-token"])),

("inference","i-l3",S("i-coldstart","Weight Loading & Cold Start",
"Autoscaling LLMs fails if a new replica takes ten minutes to become ready.",
"A new replica must get a node, pull an image, load tens to hundreds of GB of weights, initialize the engine and warm up. Each step can be measured and shortened. This subject covers where time goes, fast loading techniques, and how to choose between always-on, warm pools and scale-to-zero per model.\n\nCommon mistakes: pulling weights from object storage on every start; huge images with weights baked in and no layer caching; autoscaling on signals that react too late for a minutes-long cold start.",
[R("vLLM Run:ai Model Streamer","https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/models/extensions/runai_model_streamer.html"),R("safetensors docs","https://huggingface.co/docs/safetensors/index"),R("Knative scale to zero","https://knative.dev/docs/serving/autoscaling/scale-to-zero/"),R("KServe docs","https://kserve.github.io/website/")],
"Measure each cold-start phase for a 7B-70B model: node ready, image pull, weight load (object store vs local NVMe vs streamer), engine init, first token. Then cut total time by at least half and document which change mattered most.",
[T("i-cs-where","Where the Time Goes","Break cold start into phases and time each: node provisioning (cloud can take minutes), image pull (CUDA images are often 10+ GB), weight download and load into GPU memory (model bytes ÷ bandwidth), engine initialization (KV cache allocation, CUDA graph capture, kernel compilation such as torch.compile), and warmup requests. Without measurement, teams optimize the wrong phase. Log timestamps for each phase on every start and keep the distribution, not just one run.",["Node provisioning","Image pull","Weight transfer and load","Engine init and CUDA graphs","Phase-level timing"]),
 T("i-cs-fast","Fast Loading Techniques","Shorten the big phases. Keep weights on node-local NVMe or a shared high-throughput cache rather than object storage. Use safetensors so tensors can be memory-mapped and streamed. Stream tensors concurrently straight to GPU memory. Pre-shard checkpoints to your tensor-parallel layout so each GPU reads only its slice. Keep images slim and pre-pulled on nodes; use lazy-loading image formats. Cache compiled artifacts (CUDA graphs, compile caches) where your engine supports it.",["Local NVMe and caches","safetensors and mmap","Parallel streaming to GPU","Pre-sharded checkpoints","Image slimming and pre-pull"]),
 T("i-cs-policy","Scale-to-Zero vs Warm Pools","Every model needs a readiness policy. Hot models stay always-on with headroom. Warm models keep a small pool of ready replicas and scale out from there. Cold models scale to zero and accept a first-request delay, shown honestly in the UI. Scale on leading signals (queue depth, request rate trends) and pre-scale for known peaks. For multi-model platforms, multiplex many rarely used models or LoRA adapters on shared replicas instead of dedicating GPUs to each.",["Hot / warm / cold tiers","Leading autoscale signals","Scheduled pre-scaling","Model multiplexing","User-facing loading states"])],
[Q("Time to load 140 GB of weights at 7 GB/s is about…",["2 s","20 s","200 s","2,000 s"],1,"140 ÷ 7 = 20 seconds."),
 Q("Pre-sharding checkpoints to the TP layout helps because…",["It shrinks the model","Each GPU reads only its slice","It removes CUDA","It improves accuracy"],1,"Less data per GPU, loaded in parallel."),
 Q("Scale-to-zero is a good fit for…",["Your busiest model","Rarely used models where a first-request delay is acceptable","Strict-SLO chat","Training"],1,"Savings outweigh occasional cold starts."),
 Q("Why is safetensors faster to load than pickle checkpoints?",["It is compressed","It can be memory-mapped without code execution","It uses CPUs","It skips weights"],1,"Raw tensor layout enables zero-copy mapping.")],
["cold-start"])),

("inference","i-l3",S("i-gateway","LLM Gateways & Inference Routing",
"Routing decides cost, latency and resilience before a single token is generated.",
"Gateways give every app one endpoint with keys, budgets, policy and traces, and route across providers, models and replicas. Inside a self-hosted fleet, KV- and prefix-aware routing can cut prefill work dramatically. This subject covers gateway functions, endpoint picking, and multi-model policies.\n\nCommon mistakes: round-robin across LLM replicas; fallbacks to models never evaluated with your prompts; gateways without per-tenant budgets.",
[R("Gateway API Inference Extension","https://gateway-api-inference-extension.sigs.k8s.io/"),R("LiteLLM docs","https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/"),R("Envoy AI Gateway","https://aigateway.envoyproxy.io/"),R("llm-d","https://llm-d.ai/"),R("NVIDIA Dynamo (GitHub)","https://github.com/ai-dynamo/dynamo")],
"Put LiteLLM or Envoy AI Gateway in front of two self-hosted replicas and one external provider. Add per-team budgets and a fallback. Then compare round-robin against prefix-aware routing on a workload with a shared 4k-token system prompt, measuring TTFT and GPU prefill time.",
[T("i-gw-func","Gateway Functions","A production gateway terminates auth, maps virtual keys to provider keys, enforces rate limits and budgets per team, normalizes request formats, runs policy hooks (PII redaction, injection screening, content rules), applies retries, fallbacks and hedging, and emits traces with tokens and cost. It is also where you implement model aliases ('support-default' points to a specific model version) so apps never hard-code model names.",["Virtual keys and budgets","Format normalization","Policy hooks","Retries, fallbacks, hedging","Model aliases"]),
 T("i-gw-route","KV- and Prefix-Aware Routing","LLM replicas are stateful: each holds a KV cache. A smart endpoint picker scores replicas on queue length, KV utilization, loaded LoRA adapters and prefix-cache affinity, sending requests that share a prefix to the replica that already cached it. This avoids recomputing long system prompts and documents. Balance affinity with load to avoid hot spots. The Kubernetes Gateway API Inference Extension standardizes this pattern; llm-d and Dynamo implement it with cache-aware schedulers.",["Why round-robin fails","Queue and KV-based scoring","Prefix-cache affinity","LoRA-aware routing","Hot-spot balancing"]),
 T("i-gw-policy","Multi-Model & Multi-Provider Policy","Routing policy decides which model serves a request: by tier, task, cost budget, region or a learned difficulty score. Fallback chains handle provider outages and rate limits. Every target in a chain must be evaluated with your prompts, or failover silently lowers quality. Log the routing decision with each request so cost and quality can be attributed. Review policies regularly as prices and models change.",["Tier and task routing","Fallback chains","Eval parity for fallbacks","Routing decision logs","Policy reviews"])],
[Q("Prefix-aware routing saves mainly…",["Output tokens","Prefill compute for shared prompt prefixes","Disk space","Network egress"],1,"Cached KV for the prefix is reused."),
 Q("Model aliases in a gateway let you…",["Hide costs","Swap the underlying model without app changes","Skip auth","Train models"],1,"Apps call a stable name."),
 Q("A fallback model that was never evaluated with your prompts risks…",["Higher uptime","Silent quality drops during failover","Lower cost always","Nothing"],1,"Prompts tuned for one model may fail on another."),
 Q("Why is round-robin a poor fit for LLM replicas?",["It is too complex","It ignores KV state, queue depth and cached prefixes","It needs GPUs","It breaks TLS"],1,"Replicas are stateful and unevenly loaded.")],
["llm-gateway-routing"])),

("engineer","e-l2",S("e-vector","Vector Search Internals",
"Retrieval quality and cost depend on how the index actually finds neighbors.",
"Vector databases use approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) indexes such as HNSW, IVF and product quantization. Understanding their knobs lets you trade recall, latency and memory deliberately, size infrastructure, and avoid pitfalls such as filters that destroy recall.\n\nCommon mistakes: never measuring recall against exact search; post-filtering ANN results by tenant; re-embedding without re-indexing; choosing a vector DB before knowing data size and filter needs.",
[R("HNSW paper (Malkov & Yashunin)","https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09320"),R("Faiss wiki","https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki"),R("pgvector","https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector")],
"Index 1M vectors with HNSW and IVF-PQ in Faiss. Plot recall@10 vs latency for several efSearch and nprobe values, measure memory, then add a tenant filter and observe recall with pre- vs post-filtering.",
[T("e-vec-ann","ANN Algorithms","Exact search is linear in corpus size. HNSW navigates a layered proximity graph and offers excellent recall and latency at the cost of memory (vectors plus links). IVF partitions vectors into clusters and searches only the closest nprobe lists, which is cheaper in memory and easy to shard. Product quantization compresses vectors into compact codes, enabling billions of vectors in RAM, usually with a full-precision rerank of top candidates. DiskANN-style indexes keep most data on SSD.",["Exact vs approximate search","HNSW graphs (M, ef)","IVF lists and nprobe","Product quantization","Disk-based indexes"]),
 T("e-vec-ops","Operating Vector Indexes","Measure recall@k against exact search on a sample whenever you change parameters or data. Size memory as vectors plus index overhead. Filters by tenant or ACL should be applied during search (filtered HNSW, partitioned indexes) rather than after, or results can come back nearly empty. Plan re-indexing when embedding models change and handle deletes, which some graph indexes support poorly. Hybrid keyword plus vector search is often the best default.",["Recall measurement","Memory sizing","Filtered search strategies","Re-indexing and deletes","Hybrid search"])],
[Q("Increasing HNSW efSearch generally…",["Lowers recall","Raises recall and latency","Reduces memory","Deletes vectors"],1,"A wider search explores more candidates."),
 Q("Product quantization mainly reduces…",["Recall to zero","Memory per vector","Query count","Dimensions of queries only"],1,"Vectors become compact codes."),
 Q("Post-filtering ANN results by tenant can cause…",["Better recall","Few or no results when the tenant is small","Faster indexing","Lower memory"],1,"Most top candidates may belong to other tenants."),
 Q("10M float32 vectors of 768 dims take about…",["3 GB","30.7 GB","307 GB","768 MB"],1,"10M × 768 × 4 bytes ≈ 30.7 GB.")],
["hnsw","graphrag"])),
]

